  "statement": "I conditionally agree that mandating architectural data siloing imposes non-trivial initial compliance costs on smaller developers, but I reject the premise that the current unregulated model offers them a viable path to compete against incumbent data-hoarding. These costs represent a necessary investment in establishing a baseline for an ethical market that prevents dominant firms from weaponizing their massive, opaque data moats against the rest of the ecosystem.\n\nPrometheus argues that siloing creates regulatory capture, yet this ignores the reality that incumbents already own the largest private data sets, such as Google’s multi-petabyte proprietary repositories, which no startup can hope to replicate. By requiring architectural siloing, regulators would shift the basis of competition from who can hoard the most personal data to who can build the most robust, privacy-preserving systems. This is not a barrier to entry; it is a design standard that prevents a race to the bottom where only firms with massive, unchecked surveillance apparatuses can survive. \n\nWe must distinguish between regulatory capture and structural constraints that enforce individual autonomy. When data is siloed by design, users regain the ability to revoke access or move their information without the friction of platform lock-in, which currently affects over 90% of users in the generative AI market. Implementing these standards, similar to the data portability requirements in the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), forces companies to compete on product quality rather than the volume of data they extract from their users. \n\nMy argument assumes that market competition is currently broken by data-hoarding moats and that consumers prioritize privacy when it is a structural feature rather than a checkbox. If these assumptions are wrong, and users truly prefer the convenience of centralized, opaque services regardless of the epistemic harm to their autonomy, then my proposed architectural interventions would indeed become mere bureaucratic overhead. However, failing to mandate such siloing ensures that smaller developers remain perpetually subservient to the data-processing capacity of the incumbent giants.",
